WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.165 --> 00:00:05.450
Hm. Breaking a spear shaft with a shield.

00:00:05.450 --> 00:00:06.820
Would that work?

00:00:06.820 --> 00:00:07.920
Probably not.

00:00:07.920 --> 00:00:09.510
You can cut through a spear shaft,

00:00:09.510 --> 00:00:12.040
but it takes several
blows with a sharp sword.

00:00:12.040 --> 00:00:13.140
It's not a lightsaber.

00:00:13.140 --> 00:00:15.440
Hi, I'm Matt Easton of Schola Gladiatoria,

00:00:15.440 --> 00:00:17.612
and I teach people how
to use things like spears

00:00:17.612 --> 00:00:18.820
and swords and daggers,

00:00:18.820 --> 00:00:21.030
and I also deal in antique weapons.

00:00:21.030 --> 00:00:23.870
I've been teaching people how
to use weapons for 20 years.

00:00:23.870 --> 00:00:25.210
So today we're going to be looking at

00:00:25.210 --> 00:00:27.600
some polearm combat scenes from movies

00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:29.450
to judge how real they are.

00:00:32.830 --> 00:00:35.270
OK, so stabbing the foot
there, that was really nice.

00:00:35.270 --> 00:00:37.660
And I think a lot of people
might think that's silly,

00:00:37.660 --> 00:00:40.268
but funnily enough, there's
an anonymous treatise

00:00:40.268 --> 00:00:43.705
from the early 16th century
that repeatedly talks about

00:00:43.705 --> 00:00:45.670
stabbing the opponent in the foot

00:00:45.670 --> 00:00:47.750
and stabbing them in the crotch.

00:00:47.750 --> 00:00:50.109
So absolutely it was done.

00:00:51.460 --> 00:00:53.498
This kind of distraction tactic

00:00:53.498 --> 00:00:55.873
is in medieval treatises, so I love it.

00:00:58.310 --> 00:00:59.345
OK, whoa, whoa, whoa!

00:00:59.345 --> 00:01:02.520
They're, like, flipping this,

00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:04.150
spinning this thing around.

00:01:04.150 --> 00:01:06.000
So, this is a thrusting weapon,

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:08.150
but she is twirling it around

00:01:08.150 --> 00:01:10.350
like a snooker cue in a bar fight.

00:01:10.350 --> 00:01:12.810
That's not going to have
much effect on people.

00:01:12.810 --> 00:01:14.620
It's not a good striking weapon.

00:01:14.620 --> 00:01:16.037
Thrust with it. It's a spear.

00:01:20.060 --> 00:01:23.193
Taking turns. So, this is a favorite way

00:01:23.193 --> 00:01:25.360
for multiple people to attack a hero

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:27.020
in a movie, or a big villain,

00:01:27.020 --> 00:01:29.170
is to take turns at attacking them.

00:01:29.170 --> 00:01:30.810
Just attack them at the same time.

00:01:30.810 --> 00:01:32.730
Don't go one, two, three, four.

00:01:32.730 --> 00:01:34.190
So if they are trying to surround him,

00:01:34.190 --> 00:01:35.030
they should still have

00:01:35.030 --> 00:01:36.890
their spear points forward predominantly

00:01:36.890 --> 00:01:39.860
and form essentially a
ring of points around him.

00:01:39.860 --> 00:01:41.830
Perhaps the only excuse
you could give them

00:01:41.830 --> 00:01:43.470
is they're trying to capture him

00:01:43.470 --> 00:01:45.210
rather than trying to kill him.

00:01:45.210 --> 00:01:46.840
One of the things that really bugs me

00:01:46.840 --> 00:01:48.450
about spear use in "Black Panther"

00:01:48.450 --> 00:01:51.260
is that the spears are used
more like quarterstaves.

00:01:51.260 --> 00:01:54.100
Now, not to say you can't use
a spear like a quarterstaff.

00:01:54.100 --> 00:01:55.950
You can strike with a spear,

00:01:55.950 --> 00:01:57.610
it's just not the most efficient

00:01:57.610 --> 00:01:59.680
and effective way of using a spear.

00:01:59.680 --> 00:02:02.870
If you've got a sharp
point, use the sharp point.

00:02:02.870 --> 00:02:04.660
So, I've got to be honest.

00:02:04.660 --> 00:02:06.510
I am going to give it a 3 out of 10.

00:02:10.330 --> 00:02:12.460
So, tridents absolutely
were used in combat,

00:02:12.460 --> 00:02:15.010
most famously by Roman
gladiators, of course.

00:02:15.010 --> 00:02:16.400
That's the most famous example,

00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:19.570
but tridents did continue
existing into the medieval period.

00:02:19.570 --> 00:02:21.930
The fact that you've got
multiple projections at the head,

00:02:21.930 --> 00:02:24.160
the fact that they are heavier.
This is a heavier weapon

00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:25.890
designed for fighting against armor.

00:02:25.890 --> 00:02:28.860
In that sense, that makes them
more like this poleax here.

00:02:28.860 --> 00:02:30.980
And he seems to have a
secondary crossed guard

00:02:30.980 --> 00:02:33.038
a little bit lower down,
which is quite interesting,

00:02:33.038 --> 00:02:35.940
because there's a parallel
with European poleaxes

00:02:35.940 --> 00:02:38.110
there as well, which
has a hand guard here.

00:02:38.110 --> 00:02:40.170
That's not only to protect the hand,

00:02:40.170 --> 00:02:42.600
but it's also to give
more force to the push.

00:02:42.600 --> 00:02:43.890
So if you're fighting in armor

00:02:43.890 --> 00:02:46.310
and you plant the cross, as it's called,

00:02:46.310 --> 00:02:48.290
in their armpit and you want to push them,

00:02:48.290 --> 00:02:50.240
it gives you something
harder to push against.

00:02:53.950 --> 00:02:56.010
So, one of the first things to note

00:02:56.010 --> 00:02:58.380
is how twirly this fight is.

00:02:58.380 --> 00:03:01.170
I'm not a huge fan of such turning.

00:03:01.170 --> 00:03:03.636
I mean, they turn their
backs on each other so much.

00:03:09.922 --> 00:03:11.690
So, there's a really nice technique there

00:03:11.690 --> 00:03:15.090
where he basically turns
the back end of his weapon

00:03:15.090 --> 00:03:17.999
around his opponent's neck
in order to throw him.

00:03:17.999 --> 00:03:20.990
It's sort of an impression
of some of the techniques

00:03:20.990 --> 00:03:22.880
that we do see in historical treatises.

00:03:22.880 --> 00:03:24.280
And in armor, this is a very good way

00:03:24.280 --> 00:03:26.196
of overcoming someone. They can get up,

00:03:26.196 --> 00:03:28.330
but it's not that easy
and it's not very quick.

00:03:28.330 --> 00:03:31.770
That gives you the
opportunity to attack them

00:03:31.770 --> 00:03:33.870
while they're on the
ground and disadvantaged.

00:03:33.870 --> 00:03:35.540
And you can look for
the gaps in the armor,

00:03:35.540 --> 00:03:36.656
like their armpits.

00:03:39.530 --> 00:03:41.540
There's a really nice technique there

00:03:41.540 --> 00:03:45.440
where the bad guy, one end
of his weapon is locked up.

00:03:45.440 --> 00:03:48.050
And so he swings the other
end of the weapon round

00:03:48.050 --> 00:03:49.440
to strike with the back end.

00:03:49.440 --> 00:03:52.810
And, again, that's completely
typical of poleax combat.

00:03:52.810 --> 00:03:56.100
If one end gets occupied or dealt with,

00:03:56.100 --> 00:03:57.700
then that being pushed aside,

00:03:57.700 --> 00:03:59.690
you immediately come
around with the other end

00:03:59.690 --> 00:04:00.760
or the middle.

00:04:00.760 --> 00:04:03.093
I would give this about a 5.

00:04:06.303 --> 00:04:07.880
So, "Troy"'s really, really interesting

00:04:07.880 --> 00:04:12.100
because it's one of those
rare occasions in movie fights

00:04:12.100 --> 00:04:14.130
where we see them
actually attempting to use

00:04:14.130 --> 00:04:15.700
spear and shield together.

00:04:15.700 --> 00:04:17.890
You know, the spear and
shield was absolutely

00:04:17.890 --> 00:04:20.490
the standard dueling weapon

00:04:20.490 --> 00:04:22.990
for heroes to fight with
in the ancient world.

00:04:22.990 --> 00:04:25.650
And you only have to look
at ancient Greek vases

00:04:25.650 --> 00:04:27.740
to see the two heroes standing
in front of each other

00:04:27.740 --> 00:04:30.042
with the spear raised and
the shield in front of them.

00:04:31.658 --> 00:04:33.830
Achilles lances his spear out

00:04:33.830 --> 00:04:35.200
at maximum length with a thrust.

00:04:35.200 --> 00:04:37.320
And that is predominantly how spears

00:04:37.320 --> 00:04:38.630
are supposed to be used.

00:04:38.630 --> 00:04:40.880
You grip it as near to
the back of the shaft

00:04:40.880 --> 00:04:41.790
as you possibly can.

00:04:41.790 --> 00:04:43.400
It's actually remarkably difficult

00:04:43.400 --> 00:04:45.870
to find an opening to stab with a spear

00:04:45.870 --> 00:04:48.580
if the opponent has a
large shield, as here.

00:04:48.580 --> 00:04:49.946
That's why people used them.

00:04:54.580 --> 00:04:56.000
Really, really nice detail there.

00:04:56.000 --> 00:04:59.310
We see Achilles switching
the spear around.

00:04:59.310 --> 00:05:01.340
So he's using both ends of it.

00:05:01.340 --> 00:05:04.010
And in fact, we know in
the ancient Greek world

00:05:04.010 --> 00:05:05.870
spears often have two ends on them.

00:05:05.870 --> 00:05:08.100
Throughout history,
lots of types of spears

00:05:08.100 --> 00:05:09.390
have two ends on them.

00:05:09.390 --> 00:05:11.880
So you can stab with
the main principal end,

00:05:11.880 --> 00:05:14.780
but you can also stab with
the foot end or the heel.

00:05:14.780 --> 00:05:16.590
It's not usually so sharp or so big,

00:05:16.590 --> 00:05:18.460
because it has to stand on the
ground a little of the time,

00:05:18.460 --> 00:05:20.140
but nevertheless, if you're
being stabbed with it,

00:05:20.140 --> 00:05:21.257
it's fairly unpleasant.

00:05:23.230 --> 00:05:24.900
So, there was one little
minor detail there

00:05:24.900 --> 00:05:25.830
that kind of bugs me.

00:05:25.830 --> 00:05:29.350
Hector basically kind of
slashes with his spear.

00:05:29.350 --> 00:05:31.840
And now, with a typical
spearhead like this,

00:05:31.840 --> 00:05:34.230
it's not going to have a
lot of cutting potential.

00:05:34.230 --> 00:05:37.830
So I suppose it's fair that in a pinch

00:05:37.830 --> 00:05:39.950
you might do this to
make someone back off,

00:05:39.950 --> 00:05:42.140
especially if it's kind of face level.

00:05:42.140 --> 00:05:44.680
So it's going to make a
person flinch or move away,

00:05:44.680 --> 00:05:46.460
but even if it had connected,

00:05:46.460 --> 00:05:48.050
it wouldn't have done
an awful lot of damage.

00:05:48.050 --> 00:05:50.250
It would've been like being
hit with a stick, basically.

00:05:53.330 --> 00:05:57.043
Resting the spear on his back

00:05:57.043 --> 00:05:59.120
and thrusting over the top of his shield,

00:05:59.120 --> 00:06:02.486
this is incredibly short range
and I think of dubious use.

00:06:07.275 --> 00:06:10.570
Hm. Breaking a spear shaft with a shield.

00:06:10.570 --> 00:06:11.940
Would that work?

00:06:11.940 --> 00:06:13.160
Probably not.

00:06:13.160 --> 00:06:16.010
Look at movies where
things like this spear

00:06:16.010 --> 00:06:17.640
are chopped through with a sword.

00:06:17.640 --> 00:06:19.810
We've done experiments,
we've tried to do it.

00:06:19.810 --> 00:06:21.430
You can cut through a spear shaft,

00:06:21.430 --> 00:06:23.990
but it takes several
blows with a sharp sword.

00:06:23.990 --> 00:06:25.100
It's not a lightsaber.

00:06:25.100 --> 00:06:28.670
Breaking through one with
a shield, even less likely.

00:06:28.670 --> 00:06:30.753
So, really, spear shafts are designed

00:06:30.753 --> 00:06:32.070
to be relatively durable.

00:06:32.070 --> 00:06:34.728
Relatively light, but not super light,

00:06:34.728 --> 00:06:36.740
but pretty strong. If they're not strong,

00:06:36.740 --> 00:06:38.748
then they're not going to
work as effective weapons.

00:06:41.210 --> 00:06:45.240
We see Achilles' spear get
broken by being stamped on.

00:06:45.240 --> 00:06:47.460
Now, this is slightly more plausible.

00:06:47.460 --> 00:06:49.570
I don't think it would be
as easy as it's shown here.

00:06:49.570 --> 00:06:52.250
However, it's more plausible
than breaking it with a shield.

00:06:52.250 --> 00:06:53.384
An 8 out of 10.

00:06:57.648 --> 00:06:59.730
I know this is a very special javelin,

00:06:59.730 --> 00:07:01.560
but this is an Olympic-style javelin.

00:07:01.560 --> 00:07:03.025
It's not really a weapon.

00:07:03.025 --> 00:07:04.490
If it's the same as a typical one,

00:07:04.490 --> 00:07:08.140
it's an aluminium tube with a
kind of slightly pointy end.

00:07:08.140 --> 00:07:09.780
So this is not something that's designed

00:07:09.780 --> 00:07:11.415
to penetrate thick clothing

00:07:11.415 --> 00:07:12.670
or go through armor or anything.

00:07:12.670 --> 00:07:15.230
And I have to say, they're
not terribly strong either.

00:07:15.230 --> 00:07:16.490
I mean, if you stepped on one,

00:07:16.490 --> 00:07:18.598
you could quite easily
bend it and buckle it.

00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:26.130
She's using it like a giant lightsaber,

00:07:26.130 --> 00:07:28.700
as if it's magically
cutting through people,

00:07:28.700 --> 00:07:31.108
but I don't understand how or why!

00:07:34.620 --> 00:07:36.230
Oh, finally, a stab!

00:07:36.230 --> 00:07:37.370
Oh, a double stab.

00:07:37.370 --> 00:07:38.950
So, finally she thrust with it.

00:07:38.950 --> 00:07:40.660
I have no issue with that.

00:07:40.660 --> 00:07:43.160
She actually used a spear
like a spear eventually,

00:07:43.160 --> 00:07:46.920
but what's all the swinging it around?

00:07:46.920 --> 00:07:48.210
It's not going to cut anything.

00:07:48.210 --> 00:07:51.439
I'm going to give this a 1 out of 10.

00:07:56.250 --> 00:07:59.520
It really, really bugs me
how he uses this spear.

00:07:59.520 --> 00:08:01.740
It is the most stupid way

00:08:01.740 --> 00:08:03.400
that he could have used this spear.

00:08:03.400 --> 00:08:04.860
Basically he could have won this fight

00:08:04.860 --> 00:08:08.320
in the first few seconds if
he'd just used it like a spear.

00:08:08.320 --> 00:08:11.740
Instead he uses it like, I
don't know, like a broom handle.

00:08:11.740 --> 00:08:13.730
He thrusts with the wrong foot backward,

00:08:13.730 --> 00:08:15.500
giving himself the minimum reach.

00:08:15.500 --> 00:08:18.260
He's got enough space,
especially in the corridor,

00:08:18.260 --> 00:08:20.393
to occupy that space with
the point of the spear

00:08:20.393 --> 00:08:22.050
and keep the opponent in front of him.

00:08:22.050 --> 00:08:23.700
So the main advantage of any polearm,

00:08:23.700 --> 00:08:26.250
but particularly of spears, is reach.

00:08:26.250 --> 00:08:28.620
But with reach also comes leverage.

00:08:28.620 --> 00:08:30.160
The two things go hand in hand.

00:08:30.160 --> 00:08:31.800
A small movement at the back end

00:08:31.800 --> 00:08:33.990
means a large movement at the top end.

00:08:33.990 --> 00:08:34.870
So, quite simply,

00:08:34.870 --> 00:08:37.590
if you're trying to get
around an opponent's shield,

00:08:37.590 --> 00:08:39.500
you can go from high to low.

00:08:39.500 --> 00:08:41.960
You could go above the
shield and below the shield

00:08:41.960 --> 00:08:43.270
in the blink of an eye,

00:08:43.270 --> 00:08:45.360
which takes longer to do with a sword.

00:08:45.360 --> 00:08:47.293
The usual balance is

00:08:47.293 --> 00:08:50.690
a spear person can beat two swords people

00:08:50.690 --> 00:08:52.660
if there's no armor and
no shields involved.

00:08:52.660 --> 00:08:54.452
A spear is a game changer.

00:08:56.920 --> 00:09:00.610
Spears can block with
any part of the shaft.

00:09:00.610 --> 00:09:02.100
You can block with the tail end.

00:09:02.100 --> 00:09:03.850
Sometimes the middle of
the shaft can be used.

00:09:03.850 --> 00:09:05.070
The problem with using the middle

00:09:05.070 --> 00:09:07.290
is it means any defense
is closer to your body.

00:09:07.290 --> 00:09:08.420
But most predominantly,

00:09:08.420 --> 00:09:11.690
spears are used with the
pointy end projecting forwards,

00:09:11.690 --> 00:09:14.110
and most of the defense will
be done with the front end.

00:09:14.110 --> 00:09:16.550
The way he used this spear is infuriating.

00:09:16.550 --> 00:09:18.960
And at one point he's
even got the spearhead

00:09:18.960 --> 00:09:22.192
pointing backwards away from the opponent

00:09:22.192 --> 00:09:24.390
while holding the blunt end outwards.

00:09:24.390 --> 00:09:26.465
I mean, he doesn't even
have two spiky ends.

00:09:28.844 --> 00:09:32.140
It's an entirely metal
spear, which is unusual.

00:09:32.140 --> 00:09:33.810
Generally you wouldn't
want to make a spear

00:09:33.810 --> 00:09:34.880
entirely out of metal

00:09:34.880 --> 00:09:36.480
because although it would be stronger,

00:09:36.480 --> 00:09:39.058
it would be so heavy that it would

00:09:39.058 --> 00:09:41.250
give up the advantages that the spear has.

00:09:41.250 --> 00:09:42.910
If you make it heavy, it also makes it

00:09:42.910 --> 00:09:44.870
far easier for the opponent to grab.

00:09:44.870 --> 00:09:46.340
Although they have a lot of advantages

00:09:46.340 --> 00:09:47.890
of reach and leverage,

00:09:47.890 --> 00:09:50.070
they are way, way easier to grab,

00:09:50.070 --> 00:09:51.270
for the opponent to grab hold of,

00:09:51.270 --> 00:09:52.450
than something like a sword.

00:09:52.450 --> 00:09:55.100
A sword is mostly blade,
so it's harder to grab.

00:09:55.100 --> 00:09:57.760
This particular fight will
get a really low score

00:09:57.760 --> 00:09:58.810
of 2 out of 10.

00:09:58.810 --> 00:10:02.393
I love this episode, but
this fight just sucked.

00:10:08.350 --> 00:10:11.200
Too much swinging of the
spear, not enough thrusting.

00:10:11.200 --> 00:10:14.190
Thrusting doesn't look
as impressive on-screen.

00:10:14.190 --> 00:10:16.750
It's also slightly more
dangerous for the actors as well.

00:10:16.750 --> 00:10:18.260
Despite the fact that in the modern world

00:10:18.260 --> 00:10:20.890
we probably think of axes or swords

00:10:20.890 --> 00:10:22.860
associated with Vikings,

00:10:22.860 --> 00:10:25.780
in any army in the so-called Dark Ages,

00:10:25.780 --> 00:10:27.330
in the early medieval period,

00:10:27.330 --> 00:10:30.670
the spear and shield was the
primary weapon of battle.

00:10:30.670 --> 00:10:34.190
They fought in shield walls with a spear.

00:10:34.190 --> 00:10:36.450
Yeah, richer people might have a sword

00:10:36.450 --> 00:10:38.010
and other people might have an ax,

00:10:38.010 --> 00:10:40.220
but those are weapons that
are only really useful

00:10:40.220 --> 00:10:42.945
once you've closed beyond spear range.

00:10:44.700 --> 00:10:46.470
One of the great things about spears

00:10:46.470 --> 00:10:48.670
is they are one of the best hand weapons

00:10:48.670 --> 00:10:50.080
that you can have in your hand,

00:10:50.080 --> 00:10:52.120
and they're also a really
good missile weapon.

00:10:52.120 --> 00:10:53.650
At any moment they can get thrown,

00:10:53.650 --> 00:10:55.070
and psychologically that makes

00:10:55.070 --> 00:10:57.060
a huge difference within the fight.

00:10:57.060 --> 00:10:59.080
Even if the opponent
doesn't throw the spear,

00:10:59.080 --> 00:11:00.650
the knowledge that they could throw it

00:11:00.650 --> 00:11:04.395
at any moment as a javelin
really changes how you fight.

00:11:04.395 --> 00:11:06.350
It makes sure you keep your shield up.

00:11:06.350 --> 00:11:07.680
It makes you very wary.

00:11:07.680 --> 00:11:10.500
I've once been in a fight
in a tournament, in fact,

00:11:10.500 --> 00:11:12.610
where the opponent had a spear and shield,

00:11:12.610 --> 00:11:13.590
I had sword and shield.

00:11:13.590 --> 00:11:16.440
So I'm up there preparing
to protect my head

00:11:16.440 --> 00:11:18.850
and my torso against the spear throw,

00:11:18.850 --> 00:11:20.340
and I get a spear right in the leg.

00:11:20.340 --> 00:11:23.300
And if that had been a real
spear and gone through my leg,

00:11:23.300 --> 00:11:25.190
then I wouldn't have
been fighting anymore.

00:11:25.190 --> 00:11:26.920
Throwing spears are incredibly powerful.

00:11:26.920 --> 00:11:29.070
They will stick deeply into a shield.

00:11:29.070 --> 00:11:32.015
If they hit you in the body,
you're in big, big trouble.

00:11:32.015 --> 00:11:34.070
I mean, they'll go
through a person's torso,

00:11:34.070 --> 00:11:36.250
and they'll also penetrate
certain types of armor.

00:11:36.250 --> 00:11:38.158
I would give this 6 out of 10.

00:11:43.120 --> 00:11:46.560
So, what we see here is
the famous pike phalanx.

00:11:46.560 --> 00:11:47.640
These are Macedonians,

00:11:47.640 --> 00:11:51.110
but pike phalanxes were used
all over the ancient world.

00:11:51.110 --> 00:11:53.070
They were used by the Romans initially.

00:11:53.070 --> 00:11:54.830
It's very good against cavalry.

00:11:54.830 --> 00:11:56.030
It makes it very difficult

00:11:56.030 --> 00:11:57.900
for cavalry to operate on the battlefield.

00:11:57.900 --> 00:11:59.810
These are very, very long spears.

00:11:59.810 --> 00:12:02.010
You're far more limited
in how you can use them.

00:12:02.010 --> 00:12:04.470
You don't generally turn them
around to use the back end,

00:12:04.470 --> 00:12:07.783
although these pikes do
have a counterbalance

00:12:07.783 --> 00:12:09.370
on the back end with a spike on it.

00:12:09.370 --> 00:12:12.790
Now, that's partially so
if the pike gets broken,

00:12:12.790 --> 00:12:14.920
you can turn it around
and use the other end

00:12:14.920 --> 00:12:17.853
still as a weapon. Also, it
provides a counterbalance

00:12:17.853 --> 00:12:19.920
so you can hold more of
the pike out in front.

00:12:19.920 --> 00:12:21.550
Hoplites were quite characteristic

00:12:21.550 --> 00:12:23.750
for using the pike two-handed.

00:12:23.750 --> 00:12:25.110
They needed to use it two-handed

00:12:25.110 --> 00:12:26.970
because it was so big
and relatively heavy,

00:12:26.970 --> 00:12:28.560
but they also used shields.

00:12:28.560 --> 00:12:30.560
And so what they did is
they strapped the shield

00:12:30.560 --> 00:12:33.280
in such a way that they
can hide behind the shield

00:12:33.280 --> 00:12:34.340
to some degree,

00:12:34.340 --> 00:12:36.070
obviously protection against arrows,

00:12:36.070 --> 00:12:38.400
but also protection against enemy pikes.

00:12:38.400 --> 00:12:41.267
This has been represented
very well, very accurately.

00:12:45.380 --> 00:12:47.480
So, against arrows it's difficult

00:12:47.480 --> 00:12:50.570
because you're holding a
great long pike with two hands

00:12:50.570 --> 00:12:53.020
and you've kind of got
the shield suspended.

00:12:53.020 --> 00:12:56.150
It's not that easy to, at that
point, put your shield up,

00:12:56.150 --> 00:12:57.700
because you're not
really holding the shield

00:12:57.700 --> 00:12:59.330
in the typical way,

00:12:59.330 --> 00:13:01.570
and also the shield doesn't
cover your whole body.

00:13:01.570 --> 00:13:04.150
The problem with arrows is they'll find,

00:13:04.150 --> 00:13:05.685
it doesn't matter how good the armor is

00:13:05.685 --> 00:13:07.080
or what shields you're carrying,

00:13:07.080 --> 00:13:08.720
they will find gaps.

00:13:08.720 --> 00:13:11.440
So absolutely peppering
a phalanx with arrows,

00:13:11.440 --> 00:13:12.690
it is good advice.

00:13:17.520 --> 00:13:18.800
So, one little detail,

00:13:18.800 --> 00:13:20.220
it makes it look like the pike

00:13:20.220 --> 00:13:22.900
is traveling very, very
quickly and piercing bodies.

00:13:22.900 --> 00:13:24.870
And the one thing you
have to say about pikes

00:13:24.870 --> 00:13:26.030
is they don't move quickly

00:13:26.030 --> 00:13:28.270
because they only move as
quickly as the pike block moves.

00:13:28.270 --> 00:13:30.790
You can stab a bit with a pike,

00:13:30.790 --> 00:13:32.980
but it doesn't go through
an awful lot of motion.

00:13:32.980 --> 00:13:34.206
8 out of 10.

00:13:37.984 --> 00:13:40.417
So, it's a massive, massive glaive.

00:13:40.417 --> 00:13:42.730
Yeah, glaive is an
English word for a weapon

00:13:42.730 --> 00:13:46.770
that's essentially like
a falchional broadsword

00:13:46.770 --> 00:13:48.520
on the end of a stick.

00:13:48.520 --> 00:13:50.600
So it has the reach of a polearm,

00:13:50.600 --> 00:13:53.020
but it has the cutting
and thrusting abilities

00:13:53.020 --> 00:13:54.440
and type of a sword.

00:13:54.440 --> 00:13:56.120
And it would be a form of dao.

00:13:56.120 --> 00:13:58.420
A dao is a single-edged weapon.

00:13:58.420 --> 00:14:00.460
So it's something that's
very effective at cutting.

00:14:00.460 --> 00:14:02.760
That's a very, very big glaive head.

00:14:02.760 --> 00:14:04.835
So this is a heavy polearm.

00:14:10.120 --> 00:14:13.810
OK. I actually like the cutting
mechanics that he's doing.

00:14:13.810 --> 00:14:14.780
They're pretty good,

00:14:14.780 --> 00:14:17.380
but why have they got
to grappling distance?

00:14:17.380 --> 00:14:19.670
He shouldn't have allowed the
swordsman to get that close.

00:14:19.670 --> 00:14:21.020
If you get to grappling distance,

00:14:21.020 --> 00:14:22.520
the swordsman has the advantage.

00:14:26.395 --> 00:14:28.120
[laughs] The spinning around the neck,

00:14:28.120 --> 00:14:29.890
it's not something you'd really want to do

00:14:29.890 --> 00:14:31.250
in a real fight at all,

00:14:31.250 --> 00:14:32.776
unless you wanted to lose.

00:14:39.190 --> 00:14:41.220
I wouldn't say he's necessarily holding it

00:14:41.220 --> 00:14:42.640
in the most logical way,

00:14:42.640 --> 00:14:45.410
because he's given up all
of his reach advantage

00:14:45.410 --> 00:14:46.930
by holding it across his body.

00:14:46.930 --> 00:14:48.580
The opponent's got a sword.

00:14:48.580 --> 00:14:51.100
He's got a weapon that's twice its length,

00:14:51.100 --> 00:14:53.060
so he should be using the reach advantage.

00:14:53.060 --> 00:14:54.850
I mean, it's beautifully choreographed,

00:14:54.850 --> 00:14:57.290
but a lot of it is totally unrealistic

00:14:57.290 --> 00:14:59.390
to how you'd actually use those weapons.

00:14:59.390 --> 00:15:01.430
And that goes for the
swords or the polearms.

00:15:01.430 --> 00:15:02.669
3 out of 10.

00:15:06.370 --> 00:15:10.020
So, the bad guy has what we would call,

00:15:10.020 --> 00:15:12.120
we'd probably call it a form of halberd.

00:15:12.120 --> 00:15:14.530
So a halberd is essentially an ax blade

00:15:14.530 --> 00:15:16.330
with a rear spike,

00:15:16.330 --> 00:15:18.520
and usually they have a top spike.

00:15:18.520 --> 00:15:21.920
But if the ax blade is shaped
to have a top spike already,

00:15:21.920 --> 00:15:23.770
then you don't need an
additional top spike.

00:15:28.440 --> 00:15:30.756
Now, the way he's using it,

00:15:30.756 --> 00:15:31.964
[laughs]

00:15:31.964 --> 00:15:33.510
it looks comedic,

00:15:33.510 --> 00:15:36.970
but actually, can you think,
if you're occupying a space,

00:15:36.970 --> 00:15:39.280
how intimidating that would be?

00:15:39.280 --> 00:15:41.550
This is a weapon which weighs,

00:15:41.550 --> 00:15:43.730
probably if it was a real
one and if it was steel,

00:15:43.730 --> 00:15:45.980
probably about 6 pounds.

00:15:45.980 --> 00:15:47.880
If someone's got a 6-pound weapon,

00:15:47.880 --> 00:15:50.600
of which about 5 pounds
of that is the end,

00:15:50.600 --> 00:15:52.720
and they're whirling it around,

00:15:52.720 --> 00:15:54.420
that's really, really intimidating.

00:15:54.420 --> 00:15:56.487
One hit from that and you're going down.

00:16:01.295 --> 00:16:04.090
Absolutely the right thing
against the polearm wielder.

00:16:04.090 --> 00:16:06.130
I really, really liked it as a fight.

00:16:06.130 --> 00:16:07.220
An 8 out of 10.

00:16:07.220 --> 00:16:08.890
I would say that out of the movies

00:16:08.890 --> 00:16:09.960
that we've looked at today,

00:16:09.960 --> 00:16:11.700
"Troy" is the first one that comes to mind

00:16:11.700 --> 00:16:14.170
that actually shows
spears used like spears.

00:16:14.170 --> 00:16:17.440
I think that this is one of the
best spear fights on-screen.

00:16:17.440 --> 00:16:18.590
Thanks so much for watching.

00:16:18.590 --> 00:16:20.230
I hope you've enjoyed this video.

00:16:20.230 --> 00:16:21.880
Why don't you click on this one up here

00:16:21.880 --> 00:16:24.780
to see something else that
hopefully you'll enjoy as well?

